^Describing things simply^

In this skill you will learn how to say ‘something is’ with the word *on* (is).

Kirja on punainen. (The book is red.)
Talo on iso. (A house is big.)

Finnish does not have a word for 'a' or 'the', so the word *talo* can mean both 'a house' or 'the house'. It is usually apparent from context which one is meant.

This skill teaches nouns, adjectives and adverbs mixed with each other, but in future skills we will go through what they mean grammatically. For now, you should just learn these sentences by heart.

^A note on 'good' and 'bad'^

Similarly to English, *hyvä* (good) can have two meanings, namely 'delicicious' or 'righteous', and *paha* (bad) can mean 'evil' or 'bad-tasting' depending on if you are talking about food or about a person. The difference comes from how these words are declined. If you are talking about, for example, the battle between good and evil, you can say *hyvä ja paha* (good and evil) or *hän on paha ihminen* (he/she is a bad person) but *if you talk about food, you can never use 'hyvä' or 'paha'* as they are; these words always get a second vowel to the end when talking about food, e.g. *ruoka on hyvää* (food is good), *banaani on pahaa* (banana is bad-tasting). The adjective in question is still the same word, only it changes because food is uncountable.
[Yes, ‘banaani on paha’ does mean ‘the banana is evil’, and makes as little sense in both languages.]